  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "How to read this diagram",
      "items": [
        "Each box is a part of the system; colour = what kind (see the legend top-right).",
        "An arrow means 'talks to'; the words on it say what is sent. Dashed = background/occasional.",
        "Click a box's SRC badge to jump to the exact code file and lines behind it."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "orange",
      "title": "Complete code map (every module)",
      "items": [
        "app/engine/runtime.py - the one generic turn loop every agent runs through.",
        "app/engine/registry.py - stores versioned agent definitions and the active pointer.",
        "app/engine/routing.py - picks which topic handles a message (pluggable).",
        "app/engine/orchestrator.py / mission.py - autonomous multi-agent planning and multi-step missions.",
        "app/engine/model_gateway.py - the pluggable LLM backend (stub or real).",
        "app/connectors/ - every way the engine reaches data/tools; THE security boundary (scope injected here).",
        "app/guardrails/ - prompt-injection defence and PII tokenise/restore.",
        "app/governance/ - kill switch, quotas (checks.py) and maker-checker approvals (approvals.py).",
        "app/knowledge/ - a dependency-free TF-IDF retriever plus HyDE.",
        "app/memory/ - a bounded per-session history store.",
        "app/events.py - the normalized event bus that drives the live agent map.",
        "app/mcp/ - a real MCP tool server (JSON-RPC)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "title": "Guided tour - one turn",
      "items": [
        "1. A caller POSTs a turn to the FastAPI app (or uses the console).",
        "2. The engine loads the agent definition, governs the turn, and runs guardrails on the input.",
        "3. It routes to a topic, assembles context (memory + knowledge + tools), and calls the model via the gateway.",
        "4. A high-risk effector suspends for maker-checker approval; otherwise tools run through the connectors.",
        "5. Output guardrails re-mask per audience, the reply is returned, and events are emitted for the agent map."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "amber",
      "title": "Why the gates exist",
      "items": [
        "Connectors are the only path to data, so tenant scope is injected below the model - never from the prompt.",
        "Guardrails wrap the model (code, not prompt text) so injection and PII are handled deterministically.",
        "High-risk actions need a second approver (maker-checker); the model never self-approves.",
        "Governance can kill-switch or quota a turn before any work is done."
      ]
    }
  ]
